inline constexpr const char* kSecHelp[6] = {
    "Cutoff of the low-pass both envelope followers run through, applied "
    "before detection. Low settings give slow, smooth envelopes for "
    "modulation; high settings follow every transient.\n\nMore smoothing "
    "also lowers the envelope's overall level, so it changes how loud "
    "transients read relative to Threshold.",
    "One knob with three linked consequences: the wet path's **latency**, "
    "the splice **crossfade length**, and the **retrigger floor**. "
    "Splices can't come faster than one fade.\n\nShort is snappy and clicky "
    "at high rates. Long gives smeared, washy takeovers that cap the "
    "splice rate at a few Hz. Changing it never moves a playing grain; "
    "the new length applies from the next splice on.",
    "Gain into the keyframe waveshaper. It is applied to the *keyframes "
    "themselves*, before interpolation, so the nonlinearity never sees "
    "the sample rate and can't fold aliases into the band however hard "
    "you push it.",
    "The keyframes pass through a nonlinear waveshaper before "
    "reconstruction, which makes it an alias-free distortion. Character "
    "morphs between three shapes. CCW is a tape dropout curve. Noon is "
    "clean and fully linear: no distortion. CW is the sinc shaper, which "
    "warms the signal slightly by boosting its quiet parts.",
    "Dry/wet blend at the outputs. Full wet is classic Capicola.",
    "Centre of the bandpass inside the feedback loop. It sets the "
    "tonality of whatever the loop regenerates.",
};

